Answer: Based on the search result, traditional "pox parties" for chickenpox carried risks of serious complications like pneumonia and encephalitis, but the mortality rate for childhood chickenpox was relatively low. The source does not provide specific mortality or morbidity figures for SARS-CoV-2, but it is well-established that COVID-19, particularly in older adults and those with comorbidities, poses a significantly higher risk of severe outcomes and death than chickenpox does in children.

Source URL: https://www.dailymail.co.uk/health/article-6292683/Colorado-parents-holding-chickenpox-parties-infect-kids.html

### As of October 2020, what was the estimated herd immunity threshold for COVID-19, and what proportion of that immunity did experts believe would need to come from infection versus vaccination?
Answer: As of mid-2020, experts estimated the herd immunity threshold for COVID-19 to be around 60% based on a basic reproduction number (R0) of 2.5, but many believed the actual threshold for naturally acquired immunity was lower—likely between 40% and 50%, with some models suggesting it could be as low as 20% due to heterogeneity in susceptibility. In contrast, vaccination campaigns typically assume uniform distribution and would require reaching the higher end of that range (around 60%) to achieve herd immunity safely, as noted by experts like Marc Lipsitch and Virginia Pitzer.

Source URL: https://www.quantamagazine.org/the-tricky-math-of-covid-19-herd-immunity-20200630/

