### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted as major health organizations like the WHO and CDC do not endorse "pox parties" for COVID-19, citing significant risks of severe illness and serious conditions like [MIS-C in children](https://www.who.int/news-room/commentaries/detail/multisystem-inflammatory-syndrome-in-children-and-adolescents-with-covid-19). There was no sufficient evidence that natural infection would confer the long-lasting, sterilizing immunity necessary for effective herd immunity. Furthermore, such gatherings pose a serious risk of uncontrolled community transmission and violate fundamental [medical ethics regarding intentional infection](https://historyofvaccines.org/vaccines-101/ethical-issues-and-vaccines/vaccine-testing-and-vulnerable-human-subjects/), especially of vulnerable populations. Experts at the time, including [Dr. Anthony Fauci](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/health/2020/10/14/herd-immunity-scientists-say-trump-endorsed-covid-strategy-deadly/3655134001/), uniformly prioritized achieving herd immunity through safe and controlled vaccination campaigns, not dangerous uncontrolled infection.